\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Method comparison across datasets. Left: average prediction set size (lower is better). Right: empirical coverage (target $\geq 0.90$). SemCP and its kernel-based variants produce consistently smaller prediction sets than token-level and naive semantic baselines. All methods achieve near-zero coverage due to GPT-2's limited QA capability (see Section~\ref {sec:limitations}).}}{13}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:method_comparison}{{2}{13}{Method comparison across datasets. Left: average prediction set size (lower is better). Right: empirical coverage (target $\geq 0.90$). SemCP and its kernel-based variants produce consistently smaller prediction sets than token-level and naive semantic baselines. All methods achieve near-zero coverage due to GPT-2's limited QA capability (see Section~\ref {sec:limitations})}{figure.2}{}}
